  "findings": {
    "whole_map": "Camouflage regions and scale, all cloth/armor/buckle/support UV islands, clips, original two round lower-right fittings, black support and plain left outline retained. No new stitch dots along the left outline in v8/v9. Whole map examined before the narrow diagnostic.",
    "pattern_geometry": "The repeated zipper/binding stays on the native two-pixel pitch and original even-row phase, without a new offset row, longer extension, new detached stripe or larger hardware. The two disputed positions belong to the existing strip and existing weak gray pigment, rather than new stripe locations.",
    "y74": "Existing source gray bar is visible in the core, including sourcex209 RGB60/61/60. Its absence from the wider-box threshold maxima is not absence of a source element. v9 strengthens this existing bar.",
    "y64": "Source has a weak partial gray-bar anchor at x210 RGB56/56/57, brighter than x210 row63 RGB44/44/45 and row65 RGB50/50/50. Adjacent-column washes alter the wider/core aggregate so row65 may exceed64; this is not proof that the whole source interval is blank. In the full map and source-native diagnostic the candidate refines that existing weak strip segment on the same two-pixel grid.",
    "tone_difference": "Both candidates make these weak intervals more coherent and somewhat stronger: v9x210y64 RGB76/78/79 versus source56/56/57; v9x209y74 RGB68/69/70 versus source60/61/60. I do not claim identical local contrast. In context this remains definition of existing zipper pigment, not an added physical stripe. v9 keeps the corrected plain edge and is acceptable as faithful HD refinement.",
    "count_limit": "Whole-box31->33 and core32->33 are local tone maxima diagnostics, not a physical source-part count. No numerical equality of physical objects is fabricated; acceptance is based on complete material/UV/map review and existing source fragments."
  },
